## Ownership and drift

Every apply is recorded with the provider's `manager` (`terraform` by default). A GitOps `vulnetix config apply --prune` only deletes objects its own manager owns, so it leaves Terraform's alone, and Terraform cannot delete an object another manager owns.

Most kinds merge a document into the stored object, so a refresh only reads the arguments you set: the server's defaults never show as a diff. Import is the exception and reads every field, except that a value equal to its schema default reads as unset.

## Secrets

A secret property is two arguments: `<name>_wo`, write-only, never in the plan or the state; and `<name>_wo_version`, a number to change when the secret should be sent again. Feed `<name>_wo` from an ephemeral variable or an ephemeral resource.

## Import

Import any object by its name (`metadata.name`); one-per-tenant kinds are named `default`:

```shell
terraform import vulnetix_quality_gate.default default
```

A name the console allowed that the name pattern does not (capitals or spaces, such as `AWS Instances`) still imports and plans: the pattern is checked only for a new name, on create or on a rename. A set argument whose server copy repeats an element exactly reads as one element. A value equal to its schema default (shown on each argument as "Schema default") imports as unset, so a configuration that leaves defaults out plans clean after an import; one that writes a default out plans a one-off no-op update.

